--TEST--
a `Core` call that needs a capability compiles only where the enclosing namespace's `[grants]` line lists it: file-scope code, an anonymous function and a `use` import are all held, and an empty line grants nothing — `rule:security/authority-is-the-enclosing-namespace`, `rule:security/package-authority-is-granted-one-line-at-a-time`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// This file is in the namespace `Acme\Http`. The `[grants]` line for it lists
// `net.connect` and nothing else.
namespace Acme\Http;

use Core\IO;

autoload 'Acme\Cache' from './cache';

// Code at the top of a file has the capabilities of its namespace.
echo Core\IO::read("note.txt"), "\n";
// An anonymous function has the capabilities of the namespace it is written in.
var $exists = fn (): bool => Core\IO::exists("note.txt");
// A `use` line does not add a capability.
echo IO::size("note.txt"), "\n";
// The line lists `net.connect`, so this call compiles.
var $pinned = Core\Http::allowUrl("https://example.com/status");
echo Acme\Cache\Store::load(), "\n";
--FILE cache/Store.nvs--
<?nvs
// The line for `Acme\Cache` is empty, so code in it has no capability.
namespace Acme\Cache;

final class Store
{
    public static function load(): string
    {
        return Core\IO::readText("../note.txt");
    }
}
--FILE package.toml--
# covers: tools:packages/grants-what-each-namespace-may-do
[grants]
'Acme\Http\*' = ["net.connect"]
'Acme\Cache' = []
--FILE note.txt--
note
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0658]: the namespace `Acme\Http` does not have the capability `fs.read`
   --> case.nvs:11:6
%A
error[E0658]: the namespace `Acme\Http` does not have the capability `fs.read`
   --> case.nvs:13:30
%A
error[E0658]: the namespace `Acme\Http` does not have the capability `fs.read`
   --> case.nvs:15:6
%A
error[E0658]: the namespace `Acme\Cache` does not have the capability `fs.read`
%A
